{{#> layouts/base head-sub-title="Migrating to 10.0.0" page-title="Migrating to Simple Java Mail 10.0.0" }}

  {{#*inline "html-head-block"}}
    <meta name="Description" content="Public breaking changes and non-obvious behavior changes when upgrading an existing Simple Java Mail application to 10.0.0.">
  {{/inline}}

  {{#*inline "body-block"}}
  <section class="introduction">
    <p class="wide">
      Upgrading will mostly mean following a few compiler errors. If you used <code class="inline language-java">EmailBuilder</code>,
      <code class="inline language-java">MailerBuilder</code>, or <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader</code> directly, those calls need a
      small rewrite. You now create one <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object for your application and get builders from there.
      You still use the same fluent builder API after that.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The main behavior change is that mail settings are read once and stay with that <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object.
      Changing a system property later will not silently change builders you create afterwards. Most applications will not notice anything else. Take a
      closer look if you reload mail settings at runtime, use several mail configurations, use authenticated SOCKS proxies, inject the Spring builder
      bean, cast converted messages to Angus classes, send converted MimeMessages yourself, or compare raw MIME in tests.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Your normal dependency remains <code class="inline language-text">org.simplejavamail:simple-java-mail:10.0.0</code>. You do not need to add Angus
      separately unless your own source code imports Angus classes. This page only covers changes to existing code and behavior. New 10.0.0 features are
      documented on their regular documentation pages.
    </p>
  </section>

